// The trading example from the AgentCore temporal-policy documentation, as Dogwood text.
//
// It is here because it is the policy `TemporalPolicy.tla` models by hand. Running the generic
// checker over this file reproduces that spec's finding without anyone translating anything:
//
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/docs_trading.dw
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/docs_trading_forbidden.dw
//
// The first says both permits are live. The second -- identical but for the approval being
// forbidden rather than permitted -- says the sell permit is VACUOUS. Two models built
// independently, one hand-written and one generated from this text, agreeing on the same result.
//
// The condition carries all three things worth modelling at once:
//
//     within 3600s        the metric bound
//     input.stock:        the FIRST-ORDER join -- not "some approval happened" but "an approval
//                         for THIS stock". Propositional temporal logic cannot say that
//     ::response          the event kind, which is what the whole finding turns on

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"ApproveSale",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"SellShares",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 3600s Anchor::Action::"ApproveSale"::response{
        input.stock: context.input.stock,
        output.approved: true
    }
};
